After ascending a rogue on Sporkhack, I finally feel like I've
succeeded with Spork. My monk was by no means easy, but because he
gained full intrinsic resistances as he leveled up, I never felt like
he really faced down the challenge of incremental intrinsics.

Polero was the second of his kind, the first having stupidly died
after running into a floating eye, after which an ettin zombie pounded
him into a pudding. But luck shined on him, as he found a pair of
speed boots on the very first level. Good times, indeed.

Sokoban presented some unique challenges. I let my warhorse, Petey, do
most of the heavy lifting on the final level, but I nevertheless came
fairly close to death a few times, and was thankful that my speed
boots let me run away quickly. One Centaur escaped from the zoo and
was causing me serious problems, so I decided to zap it with a wand of
sleep. Alas, it had ignored Elbereth and donned the amulet of
reflection, so the sleep ray bonked me. Luckily, though, I guess I'd
eaten enough elf corpses so that I wasn't asleep for very long, and
was able to run away, heal, and then chuck daggers as his horsey body
until he fell.

In minetown, I found a statue of a dark knight, its nightmare "The
Spackled Pony" (I think?) running tame close by. It was a new moon, he
had no lizard corpse and I suppose he had just prayed to make holy
water. No bag or amulet, so I assume he went straight down into the
mines. Cheeky lad, too, as he'd named his fruit, "A Nude Poloroid of
Your Mom."

In any case, I gladly took the two lamps he'd bought at the lighting
store (I assumed, anyway -- nothing but candles and a few blank spaces
in the shop) and named one. Since the other didn't take its name, I
knew one had to be magic lamp. But which? The bones also contained 6
cursed potions of water, so I trudged back up to the altar I'd
converted (without summoning an elemental of Mog, thank goodness) and
made some more holy water.

Unfortunately, despite a full blessing, the djinni decided to try to
kick my ass anyway. Ingrate. Bad idea on his part.

On the way down to Mine's End, however, I found another magic lamp
whose resident was much more accommodating, and converted that wish
into the rustproof blessed +2 Magicbane. Alas, the only mithril I
found was a cursed -1 suit of Elven mithril, but I donned it anyway,
as it was better and lighter than the chain mail I was wearing. I also
found some cursed -2 levitation boots, both of which I brought back to
zero with a wand of cancellation. I didn't wear the mithril long,
however, as one of my gifts from Kos was a +5 suit of chainmail.
Heavy, but good protection.

After retrieving the luckstone (and finding a touchstone, which I
blessed), I bought a bit of protection from the priest on the way back
up and then headed down deeper into the dungeon. Somewhere in a throne
room, I encountered a Grey Dragon who left scales along with a silver
dragon a level later who did the same. Very sweet. So I decided to go
all Martha Stewart and make my own armor. The choice was tough, but I
ultimately decided on GDSM, just so I'd not be left open to a wand of
death while using a pickaxe.

Along the way, I found a cloak of displacement, but later decided to
forgo it in favor of a robe for better MC -- a vampire took me down
two levels, which I didn't appreciate at all. Normally, I wait until
I've leveled up to 14 naturally, but I was tired of waiting, and
losing level 13 when I was already halfway to 14 tried my patience. So
I quaffed a couple of potions of gain level and headed to the quest.

I never found Ludios, but didn't need to. There were so many wealthy
leprechauns on the rogue quest, I was more than able to buy the
protection I needed. Once I finally fell through a hole to the right
place, I hit the master assassin with Magicbane, which stunned him. He
teleported away, leaving the Master Key of Thievery behind. I only had
to read a cursed scroll of teleportation and head back downstairs to
retrieve the bell.

While on the bottom level, I zapped myself with a wand of polymorph,
hoping to transform into a gray dragon capable of laying eggs ... and
I did! Three eggs later hatched into baby gray dragons, which grew up
into very formidable pets indeed: Bubbles, Thomas and Sarah.

I finally fully identified my stash and got myself crowned just before
heading down to take on Medusa and the castle. My harp was, indeed, a
magic harp, one of my favorite items in the game. Stormy went into the
pack, as I didn't expect to use it very much with so many pets. As it
was, I never even got that skill to basic.

Medusa's isle was the version that's mostly water and thick with
ravens. Once I finally got to Medusa, I hit her with Magicbane and an
elven dagger I was twoweaponing with (my silver saber was too clumsy
until I finally hit expert skill in twoweaponing.) It confused her.
She then read a scroll of teleportation. Augghhh!

Luckily, she only went down a single level, and I was able to dispatch
her. Once I got down to the castle, I put on my levitation boots, made
sure no giant eels were in the way, and took to the back door. It was
a piece of cake, really. One application of the harp, and half the
castle was charmed. I took along an Olog-Hai named Big Boy and a
purple worm named Big'un back to my stash on level 4, where I wished
up two blessed scrolls of charging, an oilskin cloak, a spellbook of
identify and a magic marker (I'd spend the last two wishes on two more
magic markers later in the game) I enchanted myself up, and headed
downward.

I held off on other wishes, as I'd already found a helm of brilliance
and gauntlets of power, though I decided not to use them because I
wanted to be able to cast spells more easily. I just went with
ordinary leather gloves, and didn't bother enchanting or fireproofing.
I treated them as disposable, and replaced or repaired them as I saw
fit. So long as I took off my shield of light and wore my robe, I
could cast spells with acceptable failure rates, most importantly
repair armor, identify and magic mapping.

I also discovered, alas, that very rusty tools will break if you use
them. In the rogue quest, I'd mistaken a pool for a fountain and
plunged in, rusting a lot of good stuff. Goodbye stethoscope. I never
found another.

Gehennom was, as usual, quite a bit of fun. Asmodeus was no problem,
thanks to a tinfoil hat (turned out to be the generic helmet) and
Orcus, as usual, got stoned. Jubilex was much less of a headache than
usual, after many of his incarnations were swallowed by Big'un the
purple worm. Those things have stomachs of iron.

Finding Vlad's was a real trial. I went ahead and cleared out Rodney's
treasure zoo (where I found blue dragon scale scales -- sweet!) and
then searched all over for the upstair with magic mapping. VERY
frustrated, I finally noticed an extra upstair at the top of Rodney's
tower -- I'd never seen THAT before. Given its location, I restocked
at my stash for the final run.

I had two possible strategies mapped out for Vlad and his nasty blade,
Lifestealer. I could polymorph into a Vampire Lord, now that I'd found
a ring of polymorph control, or I could wield Stormy. As it happened,
though, it made no difference. Big'un swallowed Vlad while out of my
sight. I had to wait quite a while before he finally passed
Lifestealer and the Candelabrum.

Rodney was a pain in the neck. He double troubled me immediately and I
couldn't hit the broadside of a barn with my wands of death (I had two
at this point). Finally, both succumbed and I headed down to the
Sanctum.

In the Santum, I encountered Yeenoghu, who is a real pain with his
magic missiles -- in Spork, you're never fully protected from his or
angels' magic shards.. Overall, though, with the aid of my pets (which
now included Bernie the demilich, soon to become arch-lich, thanks to
a blessed Book of the Dead), it was much easier than usual. The amulet
in hand, I quaffed a cursed potion of gain level and headed upstairs.

I was very fond of my Grey Dragon brood, but unfortunately, I had to
leave them behind. Bubbles got transformed into a flying jackal and
met his doom at the hand of a some monster I never saw. Thomas became
a straw golem. And Sarah, well, with monsters spawning at each
upstair, it was impossible to convince her to stop eating and ascend.
So, I tearfully left her to her meal.

The planes posed no problems, though I did have to quaff a couple of
potions of full healing on air after getting engulfed several times.

On the Astral Plane, my guardian angel showed up with mace named
Demonbane.  With a cursed scroll of genocide, I summoned up six purple
worms, charmed them, and headed off to find my altar. It turned out to
be No. 3, which nearly exhausted my wands of teleportation and death
(Rodney donated one on the way up). The combination of blue dragon
scale mail with jumping boots was definitely a winner.

Again, Derek, thanks so much for creating such an entertaining
variant. Fantastic stuff!

